-- title:
--   Triviality of a pullback twist missing the ideal supports
-- statement:
--   Let $i_2 \colon Y \to X$ be a morphism of schemes, and let $J, N$ be ideal sheaf data on $X$, each invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ lies in a basic open set $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}(f)$ for some affine open $U$ and $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ such that the ideal of the sheaf datum on the affine basic open of $f$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor. Let $r$ be a natural number, and assume that no point of $Y$ is carried by the underlying map of $i_2$ into the support of $J$, nor into the support of $N$. Let $L_0$ be a sheaf of modules on $X$ whose pullback along $i_2$ admits an isomorphism to the pullback along $i_2$ of the unit sheaf of modules $\mathcal{O}_X$. Then the pullback along $i_2$ of $L_0 \otimes \bigl((J^r).\mathrm{invModule} \otimes N.\mathrm{module}\bigr)$ likewise admits an isomorphism to the pullback of the unit, where $N.\mathrm{module}$ is the kernel of the canonical map from $\mathcal{O}_X$ to the pushforward of the structure sheaf of the closed subscheme cut out by $N$, and $(J^r).\mathrm{invModule}$ is the internal dual $\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(\,\cdot\,, \mathcal{O}_X)$ of the corresponding module of $J^r$. The conclusion is stated as non-emptiness of the set of such isomorphisms.
--
--   This is the statement that a line bundle twisted by $(J^{r})^{-1} \otimes N$ becomes trivial after restriction along a morphism whose image avoids the zero loci of $J$ and $N$. It is used in the relative Picard functor material, where a component of a degenerate fibre misses the rigidifying section and the points cutting out the twisting ideals, so the twisted bundle restricts trivially to that component.
